commentBody:
@Andrzej 

Fair enough. Yes I liked the puzzle on its own but not particularly as a Thursday.

Incidentally, my very first puzzle published here was a Thursday that by all rights needed to be a Wednesday too, so perhaps sometimes I am just a wee bit sympathetic.

@Francis

“A protagonist is the central character in a story, film, play, or other narrative whose goals, choices, and struggles drive the plot forward.”

Alex is certainly not a hero, and perhaps not even an antihero, but he is definitely a protagonist.

@Ιασων 

I can name the ‘First Ladies’ and indeed ‘First Gentlemen’ for the UK from 1980 to 2018. 

Since then it’s become quite hard to keep track of the prime ministers, let alone their other half. Doesn’t help that one recent PM was quite flexible with who their ‘First Lady’ was at any given time!

@jennie 

Waymo, maybe? You have to use Uber in Atlanta to get a Waymo car, but in other cities that's not the case.

On the other hand, you could argue that driverless cars are an entirely different business. Ditto taxis.

@Jim 

The only type of puzzle I would apply that term to is where there's very few black squares, most of the answers are multiple words, and there's multiple possibilities that fit any given clue. For me, that always involves a lot of trial and error. If I were solving one of those on paper, I would probably end up erasing a few holes into the puzzle.

But those are rare, and  I can always look up a few things if I get frustrated. I'm long past getting angry at puzzles. Getting angry in general usually isn't nearly as helpful as staying rational.

@HeathieJ  Same here...I, too, filled in SCArce for [Like time, but not space]. The NE was the last corner to fall for me, and it didn't help that I had ile for the [Landform vulnerable to riding sea levels], although islet would have made more sense, but I figured well, the word itself is tiny...😂. Never mind that it's in French! I was out of options. I was still stuck, so I looked at Wordplay and voila! (ha) SCALAR! The rest fell into place. 

Sorry to hear about all your computer troubles! I hope things smooth out for you. 

I did enjoy this puzzle; thanks, James Mattina!
